# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-shadow-path-spurious-newlines — princ/print frame output differently on AutoCAD
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feat-bricscad-print-check
#+DATE: 2026-09-25

* Observed

pjb asked (2026-09-25) for the two CAD backends to be compared on the
shadow overriding of the printing primitives. The emitted bridge picks
one of two paths, by probing the host's =defun= for =&rest=:

- BricsCAD (and clautolisp): VARIADIC shadows =(princ &rest args)= …,
  normalize replaced by the identity.
- AutoCAD: the bootstrap's FIXED-ARITY =(princ obj file)= shadows kept,
  with the walk-rewriting =autolisp-normalize-princ-call= padding the
  user's 1-argument calls.

Only the first is reachable on a host that has =&rest=, so the second
was never exercised by any test -- the mock CADs in the suite are Lisp
threads publishing status strings, and the clautolisp engine accepts
=&rest= in EVERY dialect (it only warns =[lambda-list-extension] …not
portable to autocad-2026=), so even =--autocad= takes the variadic
path.

Driving the emitted runtime under clautolisp with the fallback forced
shows the two paths disagree:

| form            | variadic (BricsCAD) | fixed-arity (AutoCAD) |
|-----------------+---------------------+-----------------------|
| =(print (= 1 1))= | =\n T SPACE=        | =\n T \n=             |
| =(princ 42)=      | =42=                | =42 \n=               |
| =(princ)=         | =\n=                | =\n=                  |
| =(print "s")=     | =\n "s" SPACE=      | =\n "s" \n=           |

So on AutoCAD every =princ= terminated the line -- =(princ "a")
(princ "b")= came out as =a<nl>b<nl>= instead of =ab= -- and =print=
ended with a newline instead of the documented trailing space.

* Cause

The bootstrap's fixed-arity shadows route through
=autolisp-emit-user-line= / =autolisp-emit-user-out=, which terminate
the line. The bridge's variadic shadows were re-pointed at the RAW
=autolisp-emit-user-str= when alfe-princ-prin1-spurious-newlines was
fixed; the fallback shadows were not, because nothing could see them.

* Resolved — alfe 2.2.92

The no-=&rest= branch of the bridge now redefines =princ= / =print= /
=prin1= (still 2-arg, still fed by the normalize walk) on top of
=autolisp-emit-user-str=, with the same framing as the variadic branch,
and keeps the file-descriptor branch intact. Both hosts now produce the
same bytes.

To make the path testable at all, =emit-run-common-lsp= gained
=:assume-no-rest-p=, which emits =(setq *ALFE-ASSUME-NO-REST* T)= so
the bridge takes the fallback WITHOUT probing. A production run emits
NIL and probes as before; this is an emitter keyword, not a user-facing
option.

Test: =protocol-shadow-paths-agree-on-framing-and-failure=
(autolisp-front-end/tests/file-protocol-tests.lisp) hosts the emitted
run-common.lsp under clautolisp twice -- once per path -- sends the
same five forms, and asserts the documented framing byte for byte, that
the two paths agree, and that the signalling form fails on both. Red
before the fix on the fixed-arity path; green after.

Carriage returns are stripped before comparing, so the assertion holds
on the native MS-Windows lane where write-line ends a line with CRLF.
